[Music]  okay  i'm going to start this video by saying  i fall into the age group defined as  millennial  i was born in 1993 the year jurassic  park graced theater screens for the  first time  snoop dogg's debut album sold 800 000  copies its first week  and women were first allowed to wear  pants on the senate floor what a year  in this video i'm going to say some  things about millennials that some  people may disagree with but i'm also  going to give previous generations the  same treatment  but most of all i'm going to talk about  why generational labels are pointless in  the first place  let's start by defining what people mean  when they say generation  a generation in this context is a group  of people born within a fairly arbitrary  range of dates  for example you're a baby boomer if you  were born between 1946 and 1964.  you're part of generation x if you were  born between 65 and 1980 or  61 and 81 depending on who you ask  you're a millennial between 81 and 96  and kids born in or after 1997 are  called post-millennials  yep millennials are so bad that the  entire next generation is defined by the  fact that they're not millennials  let's play a game i'm going to list some  stereotypes and you try to guess which  generation i'm talking about lazy  entitled disrespectful  self-centered impatient if you guessed  any generation at all you're right  george orwell once said every generation  imagines itself to be more intelligent  than the one that went before it  and wiser than the one that comes after  it and he was absolutely right  here's a quote for you try to guess what  decade it comes from  i see no hope for the future of our  people if they are dependent on the  frivolous youth of today  when i was young we were taught to be  discreet and respectful of elders  but the present youth are exceedingly  disrespectful and impatient  so what do you think which decade do you  think that came from  trick question we can't say for certain  it's from somewhere around the 8th  century  people have been lumping their children  and their elders into groups for the  purpose of complaining about them since  basically the dawn of time  does that mean complaints about any  given generation are invalid  no of course not there are very specific  and valid concerns about every group  let's look at millennials for example  we're addicted to our phones and social  media  we're more depressed than any previous  generation we have very little respect  for what used to be seen as a stable  career  and we're incredibly entitled a lot of  you watching this probably agreed with  all those  and i'm sure that at least as many of  you disagreed let's break this down  there are two big problems with these  stereotypes one  they're stereotypes they are by default  highly generalized assertions about a  very very large group of people spanning  a hugely diverse background  in fact millennials are the most diverse  generation in history  now all stereotypes are based on some  grain of truth however small it may be  yes most of us are addicted to our  phones but i'm sure we all know at least  one person who actively tries to  distance themselves from smartphones and  social media  yes we have higher rates of depression  than any previous generation but that's  partly due to the social media  and here we reach the second problem  with these stereotypes not a single one  of them is caused by some unique quality  of people born between 1981 and 1996.  they're caused by a number of factors  but mainly by the people who raised them  yes millennials have been spoiled by  participation trophies but whose idea  was that  thanks dad just kidding my parents are  lovely but seriously the participation  trophy thing is a problem  when every child is given an award for  being absolutely unremarkable  you end up with a generation of young  adults who expect to be rewarded just  for showing up  yeah that's a bad thing to expect but  the millennials who exhibit that  tendency had no choice in the matter  they were raised in an environment of  constant praise and assertions that they  can do anything they put their minds to  we're addicted to our smartphones why  because smartphones weren't a thing when  our parents were growing up  millennials got to be the guinea pigs  for a culture-wide experiment that ended  up shattering our perception of reality  and leading to drastically increased  rates of depression and suicide  it sounds dramatic but think about how  social media works  we post a status somebody likes it we  get an instant hit of dopamine  the same sneaky chemical that activates  the reward centers of our brains when we  drink or smoke or gamble  dopamine is incredibly addicting and  social media is the ultimate delivery  tool  it's easy we always have our phones on  us and it doesn't seem as dangerous as  alcohol or nicotine  as far as career aspirations go when  millennials are overwhelmingly  hamstringed by crippling student loans  and if we're lucky maybe landed an  unpaid internship during school  and when we get out our job prospects  are still no better than our high school  educated peers  the eagerness fades a little top that  off with the fact that most entry-level  positions pay worse adjusted for  inflation than the jobs our parents  landed  and require 5 years of experience the  options seem both limited and depressing  so yes many millennials have a bad  attitude but the reasons are more valid  than a lot of people assume  this isn't just a defensive millennials  each generation has to take  responsibility for the environment they  provide for their children  the baby boomers who largely held the  opinion that loyalty to a company and  putting in long hours was the surest way  to move up in the world  struggled to understand why their gen x  children were apathetic about careers  and angry at society  sure the boomers were well off but many  of them didn't make time for their  families which led their children to  strive for more independence from  corporations and a better work-life  balance  of course as with anything related to  generations this is all very general  there were baby boomers with a great  work-life balance there were gen  xers who didn't drive iroc camaros while  wearing flannel shirts torn jeans and  listening to duranduran  just like there are millennials who just  want to find a decent 9-5 job and  support their family  unfortunately this whole cultural norm  of lumping huge numbers of people into  generations has the negative effect of  creating an us vs them mentality  if i hear one more person blame  millennials for killing retail chains or  golf or home ownership i'm going to slap  them with my avocado toast  this happens with every generation  society changes  the way we do business changes cultural  norms media  job markets political landscapes  everything changes  and change is scary for the people who  are too tired to keep up  yes technology moves quickly but that's  no excuse to blame smartphones for the  downfall of western civilization  plenty of people are adapting to and  taking advantage of every new  opportunity or breakthrough that comes  their way  the bottom line is this generational  stereotyping is simply based on bad  assumptions  it assumes that entire populations of  people share the same values the same  upbringing the same likes or dislikes  the same psychological tendencies  donald trump is a baby boomer so is bill  gates  they're on opposite sides of every  possible spectrum  both very wealthy one gives 95 of his  income to charity  the other was busted for running a  fraudulent charity jeff bezos is a gen  xer so was kurt cobain they probably  wouldn't have gotten along  then take millennials lebron james is a  millennial  and so is kim jong-un besides the fact  that they both like basketball  not very much in common additionally the  idea that generations exhibit totally  different values is also wrong and  counterproductive  if a baby boomer and a millennial would  just take the time to listen to each  other rather than assuming things based  upon generational stereotypes  they could very well find that they  share some similar values  these values could be anything from  political views to adopting shelter  animals to just wanting to have the time  to relax with friends and family so  to recap in this video we have  complained about millennials  complained about people complaining  about millennials probably triggered  both groups of people  asserted that generational labels are  stupid and use generational labels to  explain things  if that sounds ridiculous it should this  counterproductive arguing about why  young people are lazy or why old people  are out of touch  or any of the pointless fighting over  inevitable social changes has been going  on for as long as we've had recorded  history  and probably much longer than that maybe  these so-called  post-millennials can break the vicious  cycle but i don't know  they seem pretty lazy if you enjoyed  this episode consider subscribing to  support the channel  if you think i'm stupid leave a thumbs  down and tell me why in the comments  below  check out these playlists to watch my  other content and as always  thanks for watching and we'll see you  next week

